---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import CssMinifier from '../../components/CssMinifier.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="CSS Minifier" description="Strip whitespace and comments from CSS to reduce file size.">
  <CssMinifier client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why minify CSS</h2>
  <p>Whitespace, comments, and formatting exist for human readability but add no value for the browser — stripping them reduces file size and, at scale, meaningfully speeds up page load, especially on slower connections.</p>

  <h2>How to use this tool</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste your formatted CSS.</li>
    <li>The minified version appears instantly, with size reduction shown.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>Formatted CSS with comments and multi-line rules compresses down to a single-line, comment-free string — often shrinking file size significantly depending on how verbose the original formatting was.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Manually minifying instead of automating it in your build process.</strong> A build tool (like a bundler's CSS plugin) keeps your source readable while automatically minifying output — manual minification doesn't scale as your codebase grows.</li>
    <li><strong>Minifying source files directly</strong> instead of keeping a separate readable source and generating a minified build artifact.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Quickly minifying a small CSS snippet without setting up a build tool</li>
    <li>Checking how much size reduction minification would provide before automating it</li>
    <li>Preparing CSS to inline in an HTML email, where file size matters</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Should I minify CSS by hand for a real project?</strong> For anything beyond a quick one-off, automate minification in your build pipeline (webpack, Vite, PostCSS) rather than doing it manually each time.</p>
</ToolLayout>
